    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1600-0870.2011.00521.x",
    "abstract": "A new and consistent convection scheme, providing continuous treatment of this atmospheric process, is described. The main concept ensuring the consistency of the whole system is buoyancy, a key element of any convective vertical motion. The buoyancy constitutes the forcing term of the convective vertical velocity, which is then used to define the triggering condition, the mass flux and the rates of entrainment\u2014detrainment. The buoyancy is also used in its vertically integrated form to express the closure condition as a CAPE relaxation. The continuous treatment of convection from dry thermals to deep precipitating cumulus is made possible through the use of a continuous formulation of the entrainment\u2014detrainment rates and CAPE relaxation time, together with an embedded precipitation scheme. This convection scheme is first evaluated with the help of single-column model simulations of specific case studies encompassing a variety of convective situations. Second, a coupled general-circulation model multiyear simulation is provided as a means to assess the model climate with respect to observations.",
    "field": "Atmospheric convection parameterization",
    "problem": "The paper addresses the challenge of inconsistently representing various convective processes by developing a new scheme that uses buoyancy to provide a continuous and consistent treatment of convection, from dry thermals to deep precipitating systems, a capability often lacking in existing models.",
    "method": "This paper proposes a new convection scheme where buoyancy is the central element used to consistently define the convective vertical velocity, mass flux, entrainment-detrainment rates, and closure condition, allowing for continuous treatment of all convection types from dry thermals to deep precipitating systems.",
    "task": "The task is to describe and validate a new, continuous, buoyancy-based convection scheme for atmospheric models, designed to represent the full spectrum of convective processes from dry thermals to deep precipitating systems using one- and three-dimensional simulations.",
